From 72f052486ad725cd0403f73de6c21da766a91a07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: overdoingism <overdoingism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 06:28:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] llama: derived kq mask for batches of several sequences
 (LLAMA_KQ_MASK_DERIVED_MULTISEQ)

The derived kq mask (V3, block 05) covered single-sequence batches only, so a context with --parallel > 1 and a unified KV
cache still reserved and filled the packed mask (n_kv x n_ubatch x 2 bytes on the device and again in pinned host memory)
for its multi-sequence batches: two slots prefilling or verifying together, one slot's prefill next to another's decode.
The reserve itself measures with n_seq_max sequences, so it always holds the packed mask in such a context.

Several sequences now derive their mask with the same predicate: sequence k of the ubatch (seq_idx) has its cells and its
tokens' [tok_lo, tok_hi] shifted by k * 2^23, so a token's window never reaches another sequence's cells and the kernels
are unchanged.  It needs one KV stream, every token in one sequence, no cell shared between two of the batch's sequences
(llama_memory_seq_cp) and positions below 2^23 (llama_kv_cells now counts the cells that carry more than one sequence,
so the sharing check costs nothing unless a cell is shared); a batch that does not qualify takes the packed mask, and decode() checks
each batch before it is split: the first one that cannot be served latches kq_mask_multiseq_seen and re-reserves once
with the packed mask (as the 2-D position latch does), so a later batch never has to grow the compute buffer.
The reserve drops the packed mask for n_seq_max > 1 on a plain cache, the attention part of a hybrid memory and both
caches of an iSWA memory.
Batches of several sequences of at most 32 tokens (multi-slot verify steps) keep the packed mask, which the reserve covers;
one sequence keeps offset 0: its inputs are unchanged.

LLAMA_KQ_MASK_DERIVED_MULTISEQ=0 restores the single-sequence rule.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
